Subject: [PATCH 07204/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-23: raising the
 induce-prompt transitions cap -- mixed result, not a clean win

Added an optional k kwarg to induce_prompt (byte-identical default when
unset, verified by a regression test) and a DEV-ONLY
CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TRANSITIONS_K env override threaded into both real
proposer classes (CodexProposer, LocalGGUFProposer). 4 new unit tests
verify the env-var default/override and that raising k genuinely surfaces
more transitions in the rendered prompt.

Live re-run on g50t, budget=250, k=20 (vs the prior k=8 baseline where
correct_changed_cells=0 for both arms):

- baseline arm genuinely improved: correct_changed_cells 0->33,
  heldout_change_consistency 0.0->0.114 -- real, positive evidence for
  REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-22's diagnosed data-starvation root cause.
- sge arm's independent generation got WORSE, for a NEW reason: the
  actual code left on disk (sge runs second and overwrites the baseline
  arm's file -- the improved baseline code was not preserved, a real
  methodology gap) has a genuine NameError-class bug -- px/py referenced
  in the action-1-5 branch but only assigned inside the action==6 branch,
  which already returned. trust_energy=inf / correct_changed_cells=0 is
  consistent with the engine crashing at call time.

Neither arm reached binary_gate_pass=True. Conclusion: raising k has a
real but HIGH-VARIANCE effect -- it can unlock a better hypothesis or
invite a longer, buggier generation in the SAME session on the SAME game
-- not a reliable fix by itself. Next steps queued: capture per-arm code
snapshots, repeat with multiple seeds to characterize variance, and check
whether the existing refactor/repair-loop path is exercised for tier 2's
first-contact induction.

